1
00:00:00,334 --> 00:00:02,628
Welcome to this introductory MATLAB course.

2
00:00:03,086 --> 00:00:05,220
My name is Phil Parisi and I will be your guide.

3
00:00:05,742 --> 00:00:13,636
MATLAB is a versatile programming language developed by MathWorks, and it is widely used by scientists, engineers,

4
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:18,692
researchers, and really anyone looking to get involved with numerical computing.

5
00:00:19,054 --> 00:00:24,900
This series is made for absolute beginners, no previous programming experience is necessary.

6
00:00:25,358 --> 00:00:34,244
We will learn how to use the MATLAB IDE, what data types and data structures are, how we can manipulate data and perform mathematic operations on it.

7
00:00:34,766 --> 00:00:40,548
And lastly, we'll wrap up with some data visualization tools for you to create plots and graphs of your data.

8
00:00:40,910 --> 00:00:51,236
We conclude with a project for you to solve, where we take data from a CSV file and use it to calculate and plot a path of a robot as it moves through 2D space.

9
00:00:51,694 --> 00:00:56,612
And of course, I will walk you through the solution to make sure your learning comes full circle.

10
00:00:57,166 --> 00:01:00,932
I'm glad you are here, and I look forward to your development throughout the series.

11
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:08,548
We need to start at the beginning with the MATLAB IDE, or integrated development environment.

12
00:01:09,454 --> 00:01:11,920
MATLAB itself is just the language.

13
00:01:12,174 --> 00:01:15,010
It's the words we type on the screen known as syntax.

14
00:01:16,142 --> 00:01:24,644
The IDE is a piece of software built around the code that offers us many features such as adding new files and scripts,

15
00:01:25,262 --> 00:01:29,840
opening existing files, changing the layout of what we see on the screen.

16
00:01:30,158 --> 00:01:34,308
We can modify the preferences such as the colors and the size of the text we see.

17
00:01:34,702 --> 00:01:39,012
All these features are built in to this IDE to make your life easier.

18
00:01:39,694 --> 00:01:41,924
The IDE is extremely modular.

19
00:01:42,254 --> 00:01:45,572
We can drag and adjust the sizes of each of these boxes.

20
00:01:45,870 --> 00:01:51,876
We can even hide the top toolbar if we want, and come back by clicking one of the buttons up top and repinning it.

21
00:01:52,462 --> 00:01:57,700
And you can even delete entire sections if you want to by clicking the drop downs and close.

22
00:01:58,382 --> 00:02:03,876
You can always go back to the default by selecting layout and then default to get back to the home screen.

23
00:02:04,622 --> 00:02:07,332
Let's start using the command window to do basic mathematics.

24
00:02:07,918 --> 00:02:15,588
Be sure you click in the command window to activate it, and then you can start to enter simple mathematic expressions and hit enter to execute the command.

25
00:02:16,718 --> 00:02:18,660
You'll see that the answer comes out as 4.

26
00:02:19,118 --> 00:02:23,524
We can do 12 divided by 5, or even 4 squared.

27
00:02:24,526 --> 00:02:30,052
You'll see we already created our first variable, ants, which holds the value currently of 16.

28
00:02:30,574 --> 00:02:34,660
It appears over in our workspace, and ants is short for answer.

29
00:02:35,022 --> 00:02:43,360
Now ants is a variable, and variables hold a value, in this case 16, and they all have a name, in this case

30
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:43,876
ants.

31
00:02:44,878 --> 00:02:51,396
Instead of typing just the raw values, we can actually create variables in MATLAB and use those to add together.

32
00:02:51,790 --> 00:03:00,868
Here I'm calling x equals 7, then y equals 10, and we can add x plus y together to get our new answer of 17.

33
00:03:01,358 --> 00:03:08,868
Now we have three variables over in our workspace, and these variables they help us by providing names for the data that we work with.

34
00:03:09,678 --> 00:03:15,524
Not all data is created the same though, and we need to be conscious of the types of data that we're using.

35
00:03:16,334 --> 00:03:24,548
We can use the whose command, and MATLAB will tell us what the class or the data type of the variable is.

36
00:03:24,974 --> 00:03:29,284
And here we see that our ants, x, and y are all doubles.

37
00:03:29,806 --> 00:03:32,836
Doubles are simply the default numeric MATLAB data type.

38
00:03:33,102 --> 00:03:35,812
They can hold very large and very small numbers.

39
00:03:36,654 --> 00:03:45,988
Before I begin typing, our command window is getting a little bit clogged up right now, and you can clear this using CLC, and it removes the text from the command window.

40
00:03:46,318 --> 00:03:55,460
You'll note that our workspace variables still exist, and we can ask MATLAB for those values by typing the variable name in the command window and hitting enter,

41
00:03:55,726 --> 00:04:00,036
and we still receive out the same value, and we can use these as such.

42
00:04:00,974 --> 00:04:08,228
If you want to clear all the variables in the workspace, you can use the clear vars command and then clc again to start fresh.

43
00:04:09,262 --> 00:04:20,708
In contrast to the double data type, we have an 8-bit integer data type, which stores much, much less data, but also takes up much less storage and memory on our computers.

44
00:04:21,230 --> 00:04:26,564
Here I've defined x as 7.89, and we get the answer right back to us.

45
00:04:26,990 --> 00:04:36,000
And then I've created a variable called y that is a different data type because I've specified that I want this number here stored as an 8 bit

46
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:36,644
integer.

47
00:04:37,614 --> 00:04:40,420
You might be surprised to see that y holds a value of 8.

48
00:04:40,750 --> 00:04:46,404
That's because integers are only positive and negative whole numbers, so it can't store any decimals.

49
00:04:47,310 --> 00:04:50,916
If I add these two values together, what do you think the answer will be?

50
00:04:52,174 --> 00:04:53,540
Here we get 16.

51
00:04:54,062 --> 00:04:58,564
Also might be unexpected, but this is because we're combining two different data types.

52
00:04:59,150 --> 00:05:02,820
X is a double and y is a 8-bit integer.

53
00:05:03,374 --> 00:05:11,520
MATLAB assumes that we want to get an 8-bit integer out, so even though this value here is 8 and this is 7.89,

54
00:05:12,302 --> 00:05:15,972
MATLAB has created an 8-bit integer to round it to 16.

55
00:05:16,910 --> 00:05:23,204
This goes to show how important it is to pay attention to the data type that each of your variables have.

56
00:05:25,038 --> 00:05:31,844
That's all I'm going to say on numeric data types, because for the most part, using these doubles are fine for many applications.

57
00:05:32,526 --> 00:05:36,484
However, there are three other common data types that you'll encounter in MATLAB.

58
00:05:37,518 --> 00:05:44,772
As we've already seen, we can assign a value to a variable using the equal sign, and this process is called assignment.

59
00:05:45,646 --> 00:05:50,084
We store a single value as a variable, and each of these has a data type.

60
00:05:50,670 --> 00:05:54,532
And these data types affect variable usage and behavior.

61
00:05:55,886 --> 00:06:00,036
Previously, we already encountered the double and the 8-bit integer.

62
00:06:02,606 --> 00:06:05,668
We also have characters, or char for short.

63
00:06:06,414 --> 00:06:12,772
These characters are text, one individual character, like the letter B or the number 9.

64
00:06:13,262 --> 00:06:18,756
We store these by adding a single apostrophe on either side of the value.

65
00:06:20,302 --> 00:06:26,052
Strings are similar to characters, but they're considered to be whole words, an entire entity.

66
00:06:28,014 --> 00:06:31,748
Lastly, we have Booleans, which we will see more of later in the course.

67
00:06:32,238 --> 00:06:37,988
These can only store true or false, which is the equivalent of a 1 or 0.

68
00:06:38,990 --> 00:06:44,004
Instead of running each line one by one in the command window, let's create a script.

69
00:06:44,686 --> 00:06:47,396
Click new in the toolbar, then script.

70
00:06:49,006 --> 00:06:51,492
We can save this file using control S.

71
00:06:52,590 --> 00:06:56,200
I'm going to call mine theory data type dot m.

72
00:06:57,326 --> 00:07:02,052
All MATLAB file names should end in dot m because that's the dot MATLAB file type.

73
00:07:05,518 --> 00:07:12,964
I've copied some text into the script, and you'll notice that we can add many lines without actually executing or running anything.

74
00:07:13,838 --> 00:07:15,492
Let me walk through this line by line.

75
00:07:16,014 --> 00:07:19,300
We saw CLC earlier, which clears the command window.

76
00:07:19,822 --> 00:07:22,724
Clear Vars, then clears anything from the workspace.

77
00:07:23,406 --> 00:07:29,988
And format compact makes sure we're not using a lot of whitespace padding in the command window when we output information.

78
00:07:30,958 --> 00:07:37,764
These are each an expression or a command, and I'm separating them each by a comma, so MATLAB knows to run them separately.

79
00:07:38,798 --> 00:07:40,324
Right here is a comment.

80
00:07:40,846 --> 00:07:46,884
Comments are not run by MATLAB, and you can create a comment by simply adding a percent sign.

81
00:07:47,694 --> 00:07:51,140
Anything that follows a percent sign is not run by MATLAB.

82
00:07:53,358 --> 00:08:02,052
Down here, I'm defining X and Y with four different data types, and we're going to add them together to see the behavior that each of these data types takes.

83
00:08:03,118 --> 00:08:09,220
In lines 5 and 6, I'm using the double data type, which is MATLAB's default numeric data type.

84
00:08:10,062 --> 00:08:15,076
This is an expression where we have a variable, a value, and an operator.

85
00:08:15,598 --> 00:08:21,220
And we can separate expressions, as we saw up here, with either commas or semicolons.

86
00:08:21,870 --> 00:08:29,092
If we add a semicolon, we remove and we suppress any output that would be sent to the command window.

87
00:08:30,062 --> 00:08:39,972
Thus line 5 has two expressions but no outputs, whereas line 6 has one expression, and this will be outputted because we have no semicolon.

88
00:08:41,294 --> 00:08:49,348
In line 8, I'm using the 8-bit integer data type, and we're still adding the same numbers together, 1 and 3.2,

89
00:08:49,710 --> 00:08:57,828
but we'll have a different answer from above, adding the doubles together, because this is cast as an integer and not as a double.

90
00:08:58,670 --> 00:09:02,884
In lines 11 and 12, we're looking at what happens when we add strings together.

91
00:09:03,438 --> 00:09:06,180
I've purposefully modified this to look a little bit wonky.

92
00:09:06,670 --> 00:09:12,356
I added all this white space between the expression and the expression separator.

93
00:09:12,878 --> 00:09:18,564
I did this on purpose to show that MATLAB does not care how much white space you add.

94
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:24,356
In fact, in line 12, I've completely removed all the white space, and this will still run fine.

95
00:09:25,134 --> 00:09:33,520
It's up to you as a programmer to make your code readable, and thus why all these other lines have nice spacing, a little bit of distance between variables,

96
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:35,012
operators, and values.

97
00:09:35,982 --> 00:09:44,420
In lines 14 and 15, I'm using the character data type, and we're going to see what happens when we add a character of 1 and b together.

98
00:09:45,006 --> 00:09:52,676
When we run a script like this, MATLAB will go through and run each line sequentially, one after the other.

99
00:09:53,198 --> 00:09:58,020
And then any outputs will be sent to the command window, and we'll be left with variables in our workspace.

100
00:09:58,734 --> 00:10:00,708
There's two ways to run these programs.

101
00:10:00,974 --> 00:10:06,724
You can select the run button up in the editor toolbar, or you can do control and enter.

102
00:10:07,822 --> 00:10:11,396
Once we run this, you can see all of the variables are now in our workspace.

103
00:10:12,142 --> 00:10:15,396
Let's make the command window larger so we can take a look at our outputs.

104
00:10:16,270 --> 00:10:19,972
When we added the two doubles together, we got the value of 4.2.

105
00:10:20,718 --> 00:10:27,748
We added those same values together as integers, and unsurprisingly, we got the rounded answer of just 4, which is an integer.

106
00:10:28,878 --> 00:10:33,348
Adding the string together gives us a funny answer, 13.2.

107
00:10:33,998 --> 00:10:39,908
Because if we put these two values side by side, we get 13.2 like this.

108
00:10:43,022 --> 00:10:49,440
And lastly, when we add the two characters together, we get something completely unexpected, 147.

109
00:10:50,126 --> 00:10:55,172
This is because characters behave very differently than strings and from any numeric data types.

110
00:10:55,950 --> 00:11:05,828
Characters utilize ASCII values, which is beyond the scope of this course, but just know that each data type takes very different behaviors,

111
00:11:06,062 --> 00:11:11,524
and it's important for you to be a conscious programmer and know which data type your data has.

112
00:11:12,750 --> 00:11:20,228
A final note when developing scripts: it's good to keep your current folder with the.m file you're working with visible.

113
00:11:20,814 --> 00:11:23,556
MATLAB also likes to create these autosave files.

114
00:11:23,854 --> 00:11:27,460
It's just in case your program crashes and you have a backup.

115
00:11:28,206 --> 00:11:34,180
Any file in the current folder is going to be usable by any scripts that you have open in the editor.

116
00:11:35,086 --> 00:11:39,844
Keep this in mind when you start pulling data from Excel files or outputting files.

117
00:11:40,142 --> 00:11:42,308
Everything should be in your current folder for

118
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:48,356
ease. That wraps up our introduction, and I encourage you to play around in the MATLAB IDE.

119
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:58,212
Become comfortable executing commands in the command window, creating, saving, and opening scripts, as well as defining variables with different data types.

120
00:12:00,366 --> 00:12:03,876
One step up from data types are data structures.

121
00:12:04,462 --> 00:12:06,960
These structures form the core of MATLAB.

122
00:12:07,406 --> 00:12:16,964
In fact, the name MATLAB is actually an abbreviation of matrix laboratory, which emphasizes how important the matrix data structure really is.

123
00:12:19,214 --> 00:12:25,120
Let's take a closer look at the robot data CSV that we'll be using to plot our robot's 2D

124
00:12:25,120 --> 00:12:34,340
path. We have three columns, dx, dy, and confidence, and thus each row pertains to how much the robot has moved in the X position,

125
00:12:34,702 --> 00:12:39,844
moved in the Y position, and then overall confidence of how much we can trust this measurement.

126
00:12:40,750 --> 00:12:46,500
So far, we've only learned how to handle each of these individual values by making them into a variable.

127
00:12:46,862 --> 00:12:47,812
And the question is

128
00:12:48,462 --> 00:12:52,100
how do we begin to handle these groups and sequences of values?

129
00:12:52,878 --> 00:12:54,724
The answer data structures.

130
00:12:56,206 --> 00:13:02,084
Previously, we've learned about data types, which is when we assign a single value to a variable.

131
00:13:05,326 --> 00:13:15,140
However, it becomes increasingly more useful to be able to store an entire group of values as a single variable, and we do this using a data structure.

132
00:13:15,950 --> 00:13:20,132
Here, each value is considered an element of the data structure.

133
00:13:21,102 --> 00:13:22,724
Our first structure is the vector.

134
00:13:23,310 --> 00:13:28,612
Vectors are a 1D, one-dimensional sequence of same data type values.

135
00:13:29,262 --> 00:13:32,868
We use brackets with each element separated by a comma.

136
00:13:33,710 --> 00:13:38,500
Important note here: vectors are a subset of the array data structure.

137
00:13:40,078 --> 00:13:41,348
Here are a few examples.

138
00:13:41,902 --> 00:13:49,124
We have a character array, which is a 1 by 3 vector, with a, b, and c separated by commas and enclosed in brackets.

139
00:13:49,774 --> 00:13:58,116
The shorthand for character arrays doesn't actually need any brackets or commas, and you can put them all together, as I have the first letters of the alphabet

140
00:13:58,350 --> 00:14:00,260
in that 1x16 vector.

141
00:14:00,974 --> 00:14:11,428
A string array, such as names, just contains two strings, and then the double array z data has three values in it, but those are each separated by a semicolon,

142
00:14:11,694 --> 00:14:13,348
which indicates a new row.

143
00:14:13,774 --> 00:14:16,196
And this actually gives us a 3 by 1 vector.

144
00:14:20,046 --> 00:14:23,588
The row separation makes more sense when we talk about matrices.

145
00:14:24,622 --> 00:14:32,100
We're still storing a group of values as a variable, and a matrix is a two-dimensional group of same data type values.

146
00:14:32,846 --> 00:14:40,292
We're still using brackets, each element is still separated by a comma, but now we have rows separated by semicolons.

147
00:14:41,262 --> 00:14:47,204
Matrices are also a type of array, and all vectors are matrices.

148
00:14:50,606 --> 00:14:51,600
Here's a good example.

149
00:14:51,950 --> 00:15:00,320
This double array here, M1, it's got three values in each row, 1.2, 3.9, and 0 in the first row,

150
00:15:00,654 --> 00:15:07,524
semicolon, 4, 2, and 5 in the second row. And we call this a 2 by 3 matrix.

151
00:15:11,534 --> 00:15:16,868
We now have to talk about the super data structure of vectors and matrices, which is the array.

152
00:15:17,582 --> 00:15:20,708
We can have one-dimensional arrays, which we call vectors.

153
00:15:21,390 --> 00:15:24,452
These take the shape of 1 by n or n by 1.

154
00:15:25,582 --> 00:15:30,480
There's two-dimensional arrays, which we call matrices, and these have the shape of m by n.

155
00:15:31,470 --> 00:15:39,280
And lastly we have multidimensional arrays, these are the four-dimensional, ten-dimensional arrays that are beyond the scope of this course.

156
00:15:40,302 --> 00:15:41,732
Here is a good visual tool.

157
00:15:42,318 --> 00:15:44,676
Arrays are the overarching data structure.

158
00:15:45,166 --> 00:15:46,660
Within that we have the matrix.

159
00:15:47,182 --> 00:15:50,756
Negative 7509 is a two by two matrix.

160
00:15:52,046 --> 00:15:54,148
And then within matrices are vectors.

161
00:15:54,606 --> 00:16:03,520
And vectors can either be horizontal, in the one by two here, the 1.2 and 3, or vertical, which is the negative 7,

162
00:16:03,758 --> 00:16:05,316
semicolon, and 9.

163
00:16:06,222 --> 00:16:10,276
This will all make a lot more sense once we start using arrays in the MATLAB IDE.

164
00:16:13,198 --> 00:16:20,644
On your system, create a theory datastructure.m file and follow along, typing in each command and expression as we go.

165
00:16:21,294 --> 00:16:28,004
When we talk about data structures, we define n to be the number of columns and m to be the number of rows.

166
00:16:28,686 --> 00:16:35,200
Vectors are these one-dimensional arrays, one by n if they're horizontal, m by one if they're vertical.

167
00:16:35,502 --> 00:16:40,804
And by this we physically mean if the values are spanning from left to right or from up to down.

168
00:16:41,774 --> 00:16:48,292
Matrices are these two-dimensional arrays, and they are m by n, and visually they look like rectangles.

169
00:16:49,614 --> 00:16:57,156
We can create data structures with brackets on either side, commas separating each element, and semicolon separating each row.

170
00:16:57,678 --> 00:17:04,420
We can also use some operators like the semicolon or functions like linspace to create arrays and structures for us.

171
00:17:05,518 --> 00:17:08,964
Let's take a look at some one-dimensional arrays, which we call vectors.

172
00:17:09,454 --> 00:17:12,036
Here I've defined cities and core values.

173
00:17:12,622 --> 00:17:20,868
Control enter to run, and you see that we get out a 1 by 2 string array here in cities, and then core values just outputs as you might expect.

174
00:17:21,326 --> 00:17:24,480
I'm adding a semicolon to suppress the output.

175
00:17:25,294 --> 00:17:29,600
This variable in array is created using the colon operator.

176
00:17:29,966 --> 00:17:31,920
The colon operator works as such.

177
00:17:32,270 --> 00:17:37,828
It'll take the first value, go to the end value, and skip automatically by ones.

178
00:17:38,702 --> 00:17:45,796
That's the default, so we could write this negative 5 by ones to 5, and we get the same answer out.

179
00:17:46,190 --> 00:17:52,484
You can also adjust this to be any value in the middle, to be skipping by twos from negative 5 to 5.

180
00:17:54,254 --> 00:17:59,428
Another easy way to create arrays is going to be using the lens space function.

181
00:17:59,822 --> 00:18:04,452
Now, first I've defined a to be zero and b to be 45 five.

182
00:18:04,878 --> 00:18:08,484
I'm inputting these here as two arguments to the linspace function.

183
00:18:08,878 --> 00:18:15,940
And then I'm asking for the number of values used between zero and 45 five to be 30.

184
00:18:16,494 --> 00:18:21,220
And if we run this, the yards to sensor array will now be outputted.

185
00:18:21,424 --> 00:18:29,476
It has 30 values because we specified the number to be 30, and they're evenly spaced from 45.5 back to 0.

186
00:18:30,670 --> 00:18:38,276
Moving to two-dimensional arrays or matrices, we have a 2D array defined here, again brackets,

187
00:18:38,624 --> 00:18:44,228
commas, and the semicolon to separate the two rows in the two by three matrix.

188
00:18:45,038 --> 00:18:54,372
And then an alternative way to define this is by using a second line, because this better visually represents how these numbers are organized.

189
00:18:54,894 --> 00:18:58,884
And if we run this, we'll see that they create the exact same data structure.

190
00:18:59,342 --> 00:19:03,876
And by structure, we literally mean the structure that these numbers are organized in.

191
00:19:04,302 --> 00:19:07,652
Here are the two rows, and then we have the three columns.

192
00:19:08,878 --> 00:19:16,100
There do exist other functions such as the magic numbers function and the rand function.

193
00:19:17,134 --> 00:19:25,024
The magic function gives us a 3x3 matrix of what appears to be random integers, and then the random nums here using the rand

194
00:19:25,230 --> 00:19:29,860
with arguments 4 and 1 gives us a four by one matrix.

195
00:19:30,542 --> 00:19:33,476
We don't always have to manually define the data that we work with.

196
00:19:33,774 --> 00:19:38,980
Sometimes we end up with data that we need to load in, such as an Excel file or a CSV file.

197
00:19:40,014 --> 00:19:42,692
We have two common functions we can use to load in data.

198
00:19:43,278 --> 00:19:46,436
One is the read matrix, and the other is the read table.

199
00:19:47,022 --> 00:19:55,504
We haven't learned about tables yet, but we have learned about matrices, so we're going to use the read matrix function and pass the argument of our CSV file,

200
00:19:55,758 --> 00:19:59,716
robot data, and then save that to a data variable.

201
00:20:02,446 --> 00:20:09,060
When we do that, we can go into our workspace and double-click on the data variable to see the data that exists.

202
00:20:10,542 --> 00:20:15,076
Double clicking on the variables in your workspace is a great way to preview the data before using it.

203
00:20:16,398 --> 00:20:22,116
Now that we have all these numbers stored in a data structure, it's very useful to start indexing them.

204
00:20:22,446 --> 00:20:28,836
And by that we mean accessing values, changing things, modifying things, the general manipulation of our data.

205
00:20:29,454 --> 00:20:38,944
MATLAB uses one indexing, which means the first element in a array or matrix or vector is considered to be in the first position,

206
00:20:39,184 --> 00:20:40,100
the first index.

207
00:20:40,814 --> 00:20:47,076
This is in contrast to other languages that'll often call this the zeroth position, but that's not the case here.

208
00:20:48,302 --> 00:20:52,068
Sally is in first, Kino is second, and Hobby is third.

209
00:20:53,198 --> 00:21:01,956
When we have two-dimensional arrays, we'll separate the index values we're looking for by a comma and provide the row number first with the column number second.

210
00:21:02,446 --> 00:21:04,676
We can also take advantage of the end keyword.

211
00:21:05,774 --> 00:21:06,948
Let's see this in practice.

212
00:21:08,654 --> 00:21:15,524
B is defined as 50 equally spaced values between negative 20 and 40 using the linspace function.

213
00:21:16,334 --> 00:21:20,516
The first value of b will pull out right here using the index of 1.

214
00:21:21,326 --> 00:21:23,940
We put the index between parentheses like this.

215
00:21:24,942 --> 00:21:29,924
I'm also asking for the last value of b by using the end keyword.

216
00:21:31,214 --> 00:21:39,012
This is the same as calling the length function of b, which will give us the total number of elements of b, and then using that as our index.

217
00:21:39,918 --> 00:21:42,628
Length of b and end will give us the same index.

218
00:21:43,950 --> 00:21:52,260
And then finally, I'm calling the indices 2, 3, 4, and 5, because if we recall, we can make arrays like this,

219
00:21:52,686 --> 00:21:58,788
and then we can use this new outputted array as the set of indices we want to get out of b.

220
00:21:59,534 --> 00:22:03,396
if we run this, we'll get the first element, which is negative 20.

221
00:22:03,694 --> 00:22:07,556
For the first output, we'll get the last element, which is 40.

222
00:22:08,430 --> 00:22:16,356
We'll get the same answer because length of b just gives us the total number of elements in B, which is 50.

223
00:22:17,614 --> 00:22:23,460
And then when we ask for two through five elements of B, we get this array out.

224
00:22:25,582 --> 00:22:35,716
Slicing is when we take a part of an existing array like B, and we take items out of it and store it into a new variable,

225
00:22:37,358 --> 00:22:45,572
Here I'm asking for the fourth, fifth, sixth, seventh, eight, nine, ten indices from B to store in part of B.

226
00:22:47,054 --> 00:22:50,468
Here, part of B contains those values as desired.

227
00:22:51,118 --> 00:23:00,356
We can delete items in an array by calling the indices of the items we want to delete and replacing them with just an empty array.

228
00:23:00,974 --> 00:23:03,844
I'm printing out the length of B, which should be 50.

229
00:23:04,270 --> 00:23:11,908
Then I'm going to remove the starting from 2 from the end, 1 from the end, and the end index.

230
00:23:12,654 --> 00:23:16,580
I'm going to set these equal to nothing, which will remove them from the array.

231
00:23:17,038 --> 00:23:21,252
And then I'm going to call the length of B again, and we'll see that the size has changed.

232
00:23:22,158 --> 00:23:26,692
Here we go from B being 50 elements to B being 47 elements.

233
00:23:27,950 --> 00:23:30,948
Lastly, we can replace or update values.

234
00:23:31,694 --> 00:23:37,380
Here you need to call the specific indices that you want to replace, the first, second, third, fourth, and fifth.

235
00:23:37,902 --> 00:23:45,156
And I'm going to replace them with equally spaced values, five of those equally spaced values, between negative 5 and 0.

236
00:23:46,862 --> 00:23:57,092
And if we run this and we look at the first, second, third, fourth, and fifth, you can see we've got five evenly spaced values from negative 5 to 0.

237
00:23:59,160 --> 00:24:04,548
Two-dimensional arrays have that second argument that we need for accessing the second dimension.

238
00:24:04,910 --> 00:24:06,212
That's that additional index.

239
00:24:06,958 --> 00:24:12,132
We're going to use the data that we already grabbed previously using the read matrix function.

240
00:24:13,358 --> 00:24:16,196
Let's output rows 5, 10, and 15.

241
00:24:16,846 --> 00:24:17,636
How would we do this?

242
00:24:19,438 --> 00:24:28,256
Let's start by defining the number of rows that we want to output, which I'm going to do with 5, colon 5, colon 15, which gives us 5,

243
00:24:28,416 --> 00:24:29,540
10, and 15.

244
00:24:32,590 --> 00:24:35,140
Then we have to specify which columns we want.

245
00:24:36,366 --> 00:24:44,256
I want all the columns, and shorthand we can use just a colon, because the default values on either side of this are 1 and end.

246
00:24:45,390 --> 00:24:47,908
And if we run this, we get these three rows out.

247
00:24:48,494 --> 00:24:54,820
We should check this though and go into our data by double-clicking it and look at row 5 to make sure that looks good.

248
00:24:56,590 --> 00:25:02,980
Here row 5 is 0.14, 0, and 0.98, which matches our first row here.

249
00:25:03,918 --> 00:25:08,868
And then row 10 also looks good, 0.19, 0 and 0.95.

250
00:25:10,542 --> 00:25:15,748
Now let's replace row 1 and columns 1 and 2 with some new data that we have.

251
00:25:16,526 --> 00:25:19,268
What would be the indices that we use here for data?

252
00:25:20,686 --> 00:25:22,244
We know it's the first row,

253
00:25:23,214 --> 00:25:25,604
and then we want the first and second columns.

254
00:25:26,254 --> 00:25:28,228
I'm going to do 1, colon, 2.

255
00:25:29,006 --> 00:25:37,700
If we run this and scroll back up to that first row in data, we'll see that now this element and this element were successfully replaced.

256
00:25:38,862 --> 00:25:43,972
You may have noticed that we have plenty of extra zeros here at the end of our array.

257
00:25:44,302 --> 00:25:45,444
We want to get rid of those.

258
00:25:46,190 --> 00:25:49,028
So let's take a look and see where those values start,

259
00:25:49,806 --> 00:25:54,340
which is at the 51 here, and it goes from 51 to 60.

260
00:25:56,462 --> 00:26:04,740
We can remove those by replacing the 51 to 60 rows and all those columns with an empty array.

261
00:26:05,614 --> 00:26:10,180
And now when we run this, we no longer have those zeros at the very bottom of our array.

262
00:26:11,406 --> 00:26:19,044
A final action we can take here on this data array would be to slice it up so we can isolate dx and dy columns.

263
00:26:20,206 --> 00:26:25,344
Here I'm isolating dx by asking for all the rows and then that first column.

264
00:26:25,742 --> 00:26:30,468
And we can do the same thing for dy by calling all the rows and the second column.

265
00:26:31,406 --> 00:26:36,612
Now in our workspace we have dx and dy, both 50 by one doubles.

266
00:26:37,774 --> 00:26:40,996
Let's take a brief aside and really talk about functions.

267
00:26:41,454 --> 00:26:47,204
We've been using them a lot, things like read matrix and so forth, and they take this general structure.

268
00:26:47,662 --> 00:26:48,772
We have an output,

269
00:26:49,486 --> 00:26:59,012
the assignment operator, the function name, such as read matrix, and then we pass in some arguments separated by commas enclosed within parentheses.

270
00:27:00,334 --> 00:27:07,812
We have access to tons, and I mean thousands of functions, exist in base MATLAB, and that's what we're using right now.

271
00:27:08,398 --> 00:27:12,964
And if you need more for specialized applications, you can look into MATLAB's toolboxes.

272
00:27:13,966 --> 00:27:22,276
If you need help using a function, or don't understand what the arguments are and what the output should be, you should type help and then the function name in the command window.

273
00:27:22,798 --> 00:27:29,476
For example, help readmatrix will provide me the documentation in the command window of how to use this function.

274
00:27:30,446 --> 00:27:32,772
This can be a bit overwhelming to try and read.

275
00:27:33,038 --> 00:27:36,580
You can also type doc and then the function name, readmatrix,

276
00:27:37,422 --> 00:27:42,212
and it's going to open up the official help documentation with examples for you to use.

277
00:27:42,926 --> 00:27:51,492
This is extremely valuable, and I highly recommend you read through this documentation as it will give you more examples and show the applications of that function.

278
00:27:52,590 --> 00:27:59,424
You can also use doc for operators such as the colon, and you'll see how it's used with some examples.

279
00:28:01,806 --> 00:28:09,060
When working with arrays, you'll see these common functions, such as sum, cumulative sum, taking the minimum, and maximum.

280
00:28:10,190 --> 00:28:17,284
Here I'm calculating a new variable called dxcumulative, which comes from the cumulative sum of the dx variable.

281
00:28:18,478 --> 00:28:23,332
dx here is our argument, this is our function name, and this is our output.

282
00:28:24,174 --> 00:28:30,948
The max function can actually give us two outputs, and you have to receive those outputs in an array like this.

283
00:28:31,566 --> 00:28:38,308
Here we'll get the maximum value found within dx, and then the index position where that value is stored.

284
00:28:39,502 --> 00:28:50,372
Here's the maximum value in dx, which is the 35th index, and then we also get out the cumulative sum, which adds up each sequential value in dx to give us dx cumulative.

285
00:28:51,438 --> 00:28:58,692
Be very careful when you begin doing mathematic operations, such as multiplying, adding, and dividing with your arrays.

286
00:28:59,566 --> 00:29:02,564
When you're interacting with scalars, this isn't much of a concern.

287
00:29:03,118 --> 00:29:06,276
Scalars are plain numeric values in linear algebra.

288
00:29:07,406 --> 00:29:13,540
MATLAB always uses order of operations, but you are welcome to add additional parentheses to enforce groupings.

289
00:29:14,606 --> 00:29:18,596
Here I'm doing some modifications to the dx cumulative.

290
00:29:18,862 --> 00:29:25,664
Maybe we need to adjust this by a value of two, take that quantity and multiply it by 100, and then subtract off 10.

291
00:29:25,998 --> 00:29:31,012
I'm just doing this to show you the layers of parentheses that you would need to perform that operation.

292
00:29:32,398 --> 00:29:34,532
Moving on to matrix multiplication.

293
00:29:34,926 --> 00:29:39,300
This is the first type that we're going to encounter, and it follows the rules of linear algebra.

294
00:29:39,726 --> 00:29:44,676
This is the default behavior in MATLAB, otherwise known as the matrix laboratory.

295
00:29:45,166 --> 00:29:48,644
Everything that MATLAB does follows the rules of linear algebra.

296
00:29:49,326 --> 00:29:54,980
And in linear algebra, when you multiply two matrices together, you have to follow this rule.

297
00:29:56,142 --> 00:30:02,180
In the example below, I've defined M1 as a 2x3 matrix and M2 as a 3x1 matrix.

298
00:30:02,670 --> 00:30:10,944
These inner dimensions, this 3 and this 3, also seen as this j and this j, must agree, and the output will be i

299
00:30:11,264 --> 00:30:12,964
by k as shown here.

300
00:30:13,422 --> 00:30:15,300
So we'll get out a 2x1.

301
00:30:16,590 --> 00:30:21,060
Again, this follows the principles of linear algebra, which you should look up on your own time.

302
00:30:22,510 --> 00:30:26,308
Type 2 matrix multiplication is something you will be more familiar with.

303
00:30:26,958 --> 00:30:29,092
Let's say we have five different speed trials.

304
00:30:29,774 --> 00:30:32,036
Each index here relates to a given trial.

305
00:30:32,622 --> 00:30:41,860
Here we did the first trial with 12 minutes as the time elapsed, and 22 kilometers as the distance traveled, and so forth for each of these five trials.

306
00:30:43,310 --> 00:30:55,012
You're tasked with calculating the kilometers per hour, and so you as a clever programmer are going to put the distance in kilometers divided by the quantity of time over 60, which gives us hours.

307
00:30:55,918 --> 00:31:00,100
When you run this, you get the answer out of 64 kilometers per hour.

308
00:31:00,910 --> 00:31:06,372
But wait, why is this only a single scalar value and not a 1x5 vector?

309
00:31:06,830 --> 00:31:10,948
We wanted the kilometers per hour for each of these five different speed trials.

310
00:31:11,310 --> 00:31:12,068
What happened here?

311
00:31:13,134 --> 00:31:21,156
This is a classic instance of us using the default operators in MATLAB, which are based around the principles of linear algebra.

312
00:31:21,582 --> 00:31:30,084
What happened here is we did that type 1 matrix arithmetic, which uses default linear algebra behaviors that we did not want

313
00:31:30,464 --> 00:31:33,124
because we want to do an element-wise operation.

314
00:31:33,742 --> 00:31:37,828
To enforce this, we have to add the dot operator here.

315
00:31:38,926 --> 00:31:44,868
And now if we run this, we get the kilometers per hour for each of those five trials.

316
00:31:46,254 --> 00:31:51,524
You may be wondering why we don't have to add a dot between the time and 60.

317
00:31:51,822 --> 00:31:59,396
And this is because the linear algebra rule for using matrix division is actually the same as element-wise division.

318
00:32:00,078 --> 00:32:02,436
You can do this either way with the dot or not.

319
00:32:03,342 --> 00:32:04,932
We run this and get the same answer.

320
00:32:05,966 --> 00:32:13,572
For more information on this element-wise approach, you can run doc and then the dot operator you want to look at.

321
00:32:14,030 --> 00:32:15,684
Here I'm looking at dot multiplication.

322
00:32:19,310 --> 00:32:24,900
Another example, let's say you want to square the distances that we have set right here.

323
00:32:26,382 --> 00:32:27,588
You can do this two ways.

324
00:32:28,014 --> 00:32:37,824
You can do dist times dist, and again, you need to add the element-wise operator here, or you can use the squared operator with just dist,

325
00:32:38,414 --> 00:32:42,308
but you need to use the dot square operator as such.

326
00:32:44,334 --> 00:32:47,556
This tends to be a confusing topic for new programmers in MATLAB.

327
00:32:47,950 --> 00:32:50,980
Please, please, please re-watch this section if you need to.

328
00:32:52,718 --> 00:32:56,068
That concludes our in-depth discussion of data structures.

329
00:32:56,622 --> 00:33:00,996
Behind the scenes, MATLAB stores nearly everything as a matrix.

330
00:33:01,422 --> 00:33:07,140
Even the simple value of 7 will be stored as a one by one matrix of doubles.

331
00:33:07,502 --> 00:33:14,308
If you are ever unsure the data type or data structure you're working with, be sure to run whose in the command window.

332
00:33:16,014 --> 00:33:24,484
Now that we understand how data types and data structures work, how to index through our arrays, and how to perform mathematical operations,

333
00:33:24,718 --> 00:33:26,980
we can begin visualizing the results.

334
00:33:27,502 --> 00:33:33,316
I find MATLAB to be one of the easiest languages to use to create clean plots and graphs.

335
00:33:33,870 --> 00:33:37,348
And hopefully, after the next few minutes, you'll feel the same way.

336
00:33:38,894 --> 00:33:42,820
Data visualization in MATLAB is powerful and straightforward.

337
00:33:43,310 --> 00:33:45,664
To get started, create your own.m file.

338
00:33:45,902 --> 00:33:48,304
Mine is called theoryplotting.m.

339
00:33:48,590 --> 00:33:56,228
Whatever you do, do not name yours plot.m because MATLAB has a function called plot and this would create a naming conflict.

340
00:33:57,422 --> 00:34:02,468
Plots or graphs or charts or whatever you want to call them represent data graphically.

341
00:34:03,118 --> 00:34:11,716
On every plot we create, we'll put the dependent variables on the y axis and the independent variables on the x axis.

342
00:34:12,590 --> 00:34:20,228
When we plot data in MATLAB, we're always plotting series of data points with lines that connect those data points.

343
00:34:21,838 --> 00:34:23,920
Everything is highly customizable.

344
00:34:24,270 --> 00:34:30,148
We'll be able to choose the line style, the marker shapes, and the color of everything else.

345
00:34:31,182 --> 00:34:37,636
If you want to learn more about the plotting functionality, be sure to run docplot in your command window.

346
00:34:38,382 --> 00:34:42,308
You'll notice that I added close all to the header that we normally use.

347
00:34:42,734 --> 00:34:46,436
This will close all the figures we create throughout this exercise.

348
00:34:47,374 --> 00:34:53,280
Our first task is to plot a wave using this equation, y equals a cosine omega t.

349
00:34:53,870 --> 00:34:59,172
We're given some initial parameters, and very importantly, t needs to vary from 0 to 1.

350
00:34:59,950 --> 00:35:07,300
We can use the cosine function, which takes radians as an argument, or we can use the cos d function, which takes degrees.

351
00:35:07,982 --> 00:35:13,444
I've defined the initial parameters here, while noting we need a transformation between f and omega.

352
00:35:14,542 --> 00:35:20,388
T, or time, is defined using the linspace function, and this will keep it as a vector.

353
00:35:21,038 --> 00:35:28,400
Here I've defined y with the same equation as above, and we can note that I used the dot star element wise multiplication.

354
00:35:28,686 --> 00:35:34,212
While not entirely necessary in this situation, it is good to be explicit because t is a vector.

355
00:35:35,054 --> 00:35:40,320
To create a new figure, first we call the figure function, this will create figure one.

356
00:35:41,070 --> 00:35:48,612
Then we add a plot to it, and the first argument goes on the x-axis, and the second argument goes on the y-axis.

357
00:35:49,166 --> 00:35:49,860
Let's run this.

358
00:35:50,894 --> 00:35:55,364
Here is our first plot, but you might notice that it doesn't look very smooth.

359
00:35:55,950 --> 00:36:03,076
This is because MATLAB is plotting data points, and when we defined t, we only used 20 data points.

360
00:36:03,758 --> 00:36:09,540
If we were to increase this to 200 and replot, you'll notice the curve becomes much smoother.

361
00:36:10,382 --> 00:36:14,756
To emphasize this point, I've added a third argument to our plot function.

362
00:36:15,118 --> 00:36:19,588
This character array specifies that I want straight lines between each data point.

363
00:36:20,078 --> 00:36:26,020
I want my data points, the marker, to be a square, and I want them to be the color blue.

364
00:36:26,958 --> 00:36:37,412
Now when we run this and we can make it full screen to get a better look, you can see each of the data points are these squares, and between the data points are the straight lines connecting them.

365
00:36:38,254 --> 00:36:41,828
Inside this figure, there are lots of customization options.

366
00:36:42,350 --> 00:36:45,572
You can click and hold to move the entire graph around.

367
00:36:45,998 --> 00:36:50,180
You can click the home icon in the top right to go back to the base plot.

368
00:36:51,086 --> 00:36:58,660
You can zoom in on a certain area by clicking the plus magnifying glass, then click and drag to create a region to zoom in on.

369
00:36:59,182 --> 00:37:01,156
Then click the home button to return.

370
00:37:01,838 --> 00:37:10,564
You can deselect the zoom by clicking the magnifying glass again, and if you hover over individual points, you'll actually see the X and Y coordinates.

371
00:37:11,662 --> 00:37:18,468
We are missing a few key features like a title, maybe some grid lines, and some axes labels, so let's go add those in.

372
00:37:18,990 --> 00:37:25,156
You can add a title to your plot using the title command with a string or character array as the argument.

373
00:37:25,710 --> 00:37:32,388
Similarly, you have X label and Y label, and then you can put the grid spaces on as well.

374
00:37:33,390 --> 00:37:38,692
I'm also showing you how to change the X limits that we show on the plots as well as the Y limits.

375
00:37:39,662 --> 00:37:49,360
When we initially plot, MATLAB will auto-scale based off of the data that you inputted to it, and you can force your X limits and your Y limits if you want to. Here

376
00:37:49,360 --> 00:37:55,492
is the new plot with our labels on the X axis and Y axes, and then our title bolded up top.

377
00:37:56,846 --> 00:38:01,380
The limits that I selected for X and Y probably aren't appropriate, so we can get rid of those for now.

378
00:38:03,470 --> 00:38:04,516
Here's our nice new graph.

379
00:38:05,134 --> 00:38:08,384
If you want to save this, you can click File, Save As,

380
00:38:09,166 --> 00:38:15,524
and then be sure to save it as either a JPEG or a PNG file if you want to use it as a pure graphic.

381
00:38:16,270 --> 00:38:22,692
You can save it as a.fig if you want to open this later in MATLAB and continue editing the figure in this window.

382
00:38:23,566 --> 00:38:28,516
You may be wanting to change the style of the line or maybe use circles instead of squares.

383
00:38:29,134 --> 00:38:31,364
To do this, check out the doc plot.

384
00:38:33,070 --> 00:38:39,524
If you scroll about halfway down, you'll see all the options for line styles, markers, and color names.

385
00:38:40,078 --> 00:38:47,024
Let's say we want upward pointing triangles that are green and have a dashed line between them.

386
00:38:47,342 --> 00:38:53,700
We'd come back to our code and change this to dash dash upwards and green.

387
00:38:54,830 --> 00:38:55,396
Run this,

388
00:38:56,046 --> 00:38:57,504
and there's our green triangles.

389
00:38:57,806 --> 00:39:00,964
Little bit hard to see, but it gives us the result we're looking for.

390
00:39:01,710 --> 00:39:04,420
We can also keep adding extra arguments.

391
00:39:04,846 --> 00:39:08,144
And what we do is we first call the attribute that we want to change.

392
00:39:08,462 --> 00:39:14,980
Here I'm changing the marker face color and then providing the value that I want to set marker face color to.

393
00:39:15,886 --> 00:39:18,500
This is one of the many ways that you can customize your plots.

394
00:39:18,830 --> 00:39:22,304
You can also change the edge color to be different than the face color.

395
00:39:22,464 --> 00:39:23,876
You can change the size.

396
00:39:24,238 --> 00:39:26,788
Everything is covered in the plot documentation.

397
00:39:27,566 --> 00:39:32,484
The next example will be displaying the minimum value of a function as shown here.

398
00:39:32,846 --> 00:39:39,012
I've defined this as a new section, however, and sections are defined using this double percent sign.

399
00:39:39,854 --> 00:39:48,900
Now that I'm running this new code below, I still want to keep my code up top, but I don't want the figures and outputs to keep displaying every time I run this.

400
00:39:49,454 --> 00:39:55,940
So to just run a section, you use control enter and it will only run lines 34 and below.

401
00:39:56,718 --> 00:40:00,704
If you use the run command button in the editor tab,

402
00:40:02,350 --> 00:40:06,532
that will run the entire.m file or all sections.

403
00:40:07,790 --> 00:40:08,996
We have three things to do.

404
00:40:09,262 --> 00:40:15,940
Let's first plot the function, then determine what the global minimum value is and make that stand out on the plot.

405
00:40:16,558 --> 00:40:21,092
And then I want to output the minimum coordinates professionally, as if this were a report.

406
00:40:21,774 --> 00:40:24,004
Here are the steps and I'll go through them one by one.

407
00:40:25,006 --> 00:40:28,228
We should note that we need to define what our x value will be.

408
00:40:28,782 --> 00:40:35,204
We're looking for the minimum value of this function, but we're only evaluating the function over this range of values.

409
00:40:35,726 --> 00:40:40,772
We also need to ask ourselves what's the number of points that we should use to define our function between.

410
00:40:41,646 --> 00:40:47,812
Usually this can be determined with some pre-analysis of the function, or you can just do it experimentally.

411
00:40:48,078 --> 00:40:50,340
Plot and see if you can find the global minimum.

412
00:40:51,438 --> 00:40:53,184
Here we're defining the y vector.

413
00:40:53,550 --> 00:41:00,420
Note that we have to use the dot xmote notation because x minus 2 is a vector, because x is a vector.

414
00:41:01,358 --> 00:41:05,412
Then we're defining figure 2 right here and we're adding a plot to it.

415
00:41:06,286 --> 00:41:09,024
I have one extra command which is hold on.

416
00:41:09,518 --> 00:41:16,132
Hold on tells this figure to wait a moment because we're going to plot something else below.

417
00:41:17,006 --> 00:41:21,188
This is how you can include multiple plot commands on one figure.

418
00:41:21,806 --> 00:41:26,980
And yes, this means that you can plot two lines, four lines, ten lines, other data points.

419
00:41:27,566 --> 00:41:31,844
Anything you want to do can all go on one figure using this hold on command.

420
00:41:32,462 --> 00:41:36,064
And when you're done plotting, it's good form to use hold off.

421
00:41:36,654 --> 00:41:41,508
I copy and pasted these lines from above, and we should rename them to reflect our current data.

422
00:41:42,030 --> 00:41:45,264
Next, let's determine the minimum value of y.

423
00:41:45,838 --> 00:41:55,268
To do this, we can use the min function, and as we saw earlier, we can extract both that minimum value and also the index where the minimum value occurs.

424
00:41:56,014 --> 00:42:05,924
Then once we have that index saved, we can go into the x vector and pull out that minimum x value that correlates to where the minimum y value is.

425
00:42:06,702 --> 00:42:14,372
Now when we use plot again, because we already have the hold set to on, we can just plot the min x and min y.

426
00:42:15,022 --> 00:42:18,116
These can be vectors here, or they can be individual values.

427
00:42:18,542 --> 00:42:22,244
You just have to provide the same number of points in X and in Y.

428
00:42:22,926 --> 00:42:30,404
This will be a magenta circle, and then I'm setting the face color to magenta, and I'm increasing the marker size to 10.

429
00:42:31,310 --> 00:42:34,116
If we run this, we're getting this display.

430
00:42:34,670 --> 00:42:43,908
We're only plotting, looks like a handful, and in fact, we're only plotting 10 values between negative 10 and 0, and we pulled out this minimum value.

431
00:42:44,334 --> 00:42:55,236
Of course, this minimum value is the correct minimum value of the data points that we selected, but it might not necessarily be showing the global minimum value of this function.

432
00:42:55,854 --> 00:43:01,764
Let's increase the range of values we're plotting with from negative 10 to 10, and let's do a hundred points.

433
00:43:02,798 --> 00:43:08,612
This is a much better graph because we've included a larger range and more values.

434
00:43:09,358 --> 00:43:12,740
Now we can clearly see that we're capturing that global minimum value.

435
00:43:14,222 --> 00:43:18,916
Lastly, we need to print out the minimum value. And there are three common methods to do this.

436
00:43:19,886 --> 00:43:22,980
The first method simply retype the variable name.

437
00:43:24,014 --> 00:43:29,412
The second method uses the display command to display the value stored in this variable.

438
00:43:29,934 --> 00:43:32,484
And lastly is the fprintf function.

439
00:43:33,102 --> 00:43:41,220
This function outputs this string, and wherever there is a percent sign, it grabs the next value here to replace this.

440
00:43:42,030 --> 00:43:48,548
Because we have two percent signs, we're pulling in both values to replace those in this one long string.

441
00:43:49,806 --> 00:43:53,604
To learn more about fprintf, please consult the documentation.

442
00:43:54,670 --> 00:43:59,876
When we run this, we'll see that we get the min y value, which comes from line 64.

443
00:44:00,398 --> 00:44:05,124
Line 65 only displays the raw value, not the variable name.

444
00:44:05,614 --> 00:44:09,796
And lastly, our fprintf statement makes this nice formatted output.

445
00:44:10,894 --> 00:44:18,468
If you're curious, the actual minimum value of the function we're using is x equals 2 and y equals negative 5.

446
00:44:19,118 --> 00:44:29,220
The reason there is a difference between this numerical minimum and the actual analytical minimum is because we're only evaluating this solution on a finite set of points.

447
00:44:29,998 --> 00:44:37,028
If we increase the number of values in our x variable, the analytical minimum and numerical minimum should become closer.

448
00:44:38,734 --> 00:44:43,620
And if we run this, we'll see that we in fact do get out x equals 2 and y equals 5.

449
00:44:44,302 --> 00:44:46,372
We just covered two-dimensional plots.

450
00:44:47,150 --> 00:44:53,668
However, as you can imagine, MATLAB can plot in 3D, it can make pie charts, histograms, meshes, contours.

451
00:44:53,840 --> 00:44:55,332
The possibilities are endless.

452
00:44:55,918 --> 00:44:58,212
But the structure of your code should remain the same.

453
00:44:58,670 --> 00:45:06,560
The main thing that will change is instead of using plot, you'll be using the name of a different function, like scatter or plot3

454
00:45:06,960 --> 00:45:10,084
or contour or whatever graphical tool you're using.

455
00:45:11,118 --> 00:45:17,636
Everything else, defining values, using hold on, putting in titles, these all remain the same.

456
00:45:18,190 --> 00:45:21,188
And thus you can use this framework with different plot types.

457
00:45:22,574 --> 00:45:26,080
That wraps up the basics of data visualization in MATLAB.

458
00:45:26,542 --> 00:45:28,804
But keep in mind this is just the beginning.

459
00:45:29,198 --> 00:45:36,720
We could do an entire course on three-dimensional visualizations, plotting multiple graphs on one figure using subplots.

460
00:45:37,134 --> 00:45:48,400
You could do a fifth-degree polynomial trend line, and actually, MATLAB has an entire toolbox called the curve fitting toolbox to allow you to fit trend lines to your data.

461
00:45:48,750 --> 00:45:52,772
Stay curious, because if you can think it, MATLAB can probably plot it.

462
00:45:54,766 --> 00:45:55,680
Job well done.

