Good luck.

485
00:47:50,062 --> 00:47:53,284
Let's walk through the solution to the robot path problem.

486
00:47:54,094 --> 00:48:03,524
We're given the robot data CSV, and our job is to clean the data, figure out the average velocities and X and Y, and then create a professional plot showing the robot's path.

487
00:48:04,910 --> 00:48:11,268
We start off with the robot data CSV, and let's review what that data looks like by double-clicking the CSV file.

488
00:48:12,462 --> 00:48:18,820
Here we have the differential X, differential Y, and the confidence of those measurements taken by some sensor.

489
00:48:19,406 --> 00:48:23,556
If we scroll to the bottom, we'll see that we have some excess values here that we need to remove.

490
00:48:24,174 --> 00:48:29,892
Additionally, we have some values of very low confidence, so we'll want to remove these rows entirely.

491
00:48:31,374 --> 00:48:40,708
The first thing we do is utilize the read matrix function, and this will pull in the robot data.csv file into whatever the current.m file is that you're using.

492
00:48:42,030 --> 00:48:45,264
Then we need to remove the excess values at the end of the file.

493
00:48:45,550 --> 00:48:51,140
We'll call the rows 51 to 60, all the columns, and replace that with an empty matrix.

494
00:48:52,142 --> 00:48:57,664
We are then told that we're missing the time element of our data, and we need to add this in.

495
00:48:57,838 --> 00:49:00,036
And total 20 seconds have elapsed.

496
00:49:01,486 --> 00:49:07,824
We're going to create a fourth column of the data matrix, and this should be from 0 to 20 seconds.

497
00:49:09,070 --> 00:49:17,892
I'm calling out all the rows and the fourth column to add this to data, and we're going to use the linspace function going from 0 to 20.

498
00:49:18,894 --> 00:49:27,236
We want every single column in our data matrix to have the same number of rows, and thus I'm calling the length of data,

499
00:49:27,534 --> 00:49:34,372
which will give us the current number of rows, and we'll use this to define the number of values between 0 and 20.

500
00:49:35,310 --> 00:49:36,964
Then we have some data to correct.

501
00:49:37,390 --> 00:49:43,332
We're going to replace row 1, columns 1 and 2 with this new matrix of values.

502
00:49:44,142 --> 00:49:46,340
There are two rows of low confidence.

503
00:49:46,766 --> 00:49:50,308
This indicates a bad sensor measurement, so we will throw those out.

504
00:49:51,662 --> 00:49:55,556
We call the indices 27 and 35 here in a matrix,

505
00:49:56,558 --> 00:49:59,684
all the columns, and replace it with an empty matrix.

506
00:50:00,558 --> 00:50:04,196
When we run this, our data is now a 48 by 4 double.

507
00:50:04,846 --> 00:50:14,020
We removed 10 extra rows that were excess zeros, we took out two other rows that were low confidence, and then we added in that fourth column for time.

508
00:50:15,726 --> 00:50:20,996
Next, we are asked to separate the data matrix into four separate variables.

509
00:50:21,326 --> 00:50:22,852
We can do this with slicing.

510
00:50:23,310 --> 00:50:28,868
We specify data and call out all the rows to keep and only the column that we want.

511
00:50:29,966 --> 00:50:38,144
Next, to calculate the average velocities in the x and y dimensions, we should do the dx over dt,

512
00:50:38,318 --> 00:50:43,876
or dy over dt, which is the differential or incremental changes in those variables.

513
00:50:44,622 --> 00:50:50,948
We already have the change in x, which is the dx variable here, but how do we get the change in time?

514
00:50:51,406 --> 00:51:00,292
We can do this using the diff function, which goes through this vector and takes the difference between each pair of corresponding elements.

515
00:51:01,070 --> 00:51:07,972
However, when we take the difference of each of the 48 elements here, we'll only get 47 differences.

516
00:51:08,750 --> 00:51:16,132
When we do element-wise division of two vectors, they need to be of the same length, so that we can divide each element by another.

517
00:51:16,622 --> 00:51:23,908
Thus, we have to specify that we're only going to do one to the n minus one of the dx values.

518
00:51:24,942 --> 00:51:30,532
This ensures that the numerator has 47 values and the denominator has 47 values.

519
00:51:31,118 --> 00:51:39,556
This will give us the vx variable, and if we take the average of the vx variable using the mean function, we end up with vx average.

520
00:51:40,174 --> 00:51:44,548
I'm using the fprintf statement to make a nice output to the command window.

521
00:51:45,390 --> 00:51:49,380
The same process applies for the velocity in the y direction.

522
00:51:50,222 --> 00:51:58,756
After running this, we end up with more variables in our workspace, and we also have our nice outputs of the average velocities in the x and y directions.

523
00:51:59,726 --> 00:52:05,924
The final requirement is creating a plot that shows the cumulative position of the robot in 2D space.

524
00:52:06,542 --> 00:52:10,596
We want to mark the beginning with a green circle and the end with a red square.

525
00:52:11,022 --> 00:52:18,564
The first thing we need to do is to create the x and y cumulative vectors, which we can do with the cumulative sum function.

526
00:52:19,406 --> 00:52:26,148
Next we create a figure one and we plot the x and y cumulative using blue circles.

527
00:52:27,246 --> 00:52:32,612
I'm calling the hold on command because I know I want to plot this green circle and red square next.

528
00:52:33,710 --> 00:52:37,572
I'm plotting the first index of the x and the y variables,

529
00:52:38,222 --> 00:52:42,404
marking them with the appropriate color, and then filling in those markers.

530
00:52:43,214 --> 00:52:50,468
I do the same thing for the last value or the end value, marking them with the appropriate colors and filling them in.

531
00:52:51,246 --> 00:53:00,068
Of course, we always want to add a title, X and Y labels, put on the grid, and then for good form we'll call the hold off that we started right here.

532
00:53:01,038 --> 00:53:03,908
Adding a legend is very straightforward with the legend command.

533
00:53:04,558 --> 00:53:08,388
The order that you plot things will match the order of these arguments.

534
00:53:08,910 --> 00:53:17,184
Hence why the path is the first plot, which was created here, and then we plotted the first value, the start, and then the end,

535
00:53:17,614 --> 00:53:18,532
which will match end.

536
00:53:19,566 --> 00:53:27,140
I'm also giving a location argument and putting it in the southwest so our legend appears at the bottom left of our figure.

537
00:53:28,046 --> 00:53:35,204
As a bonus, we're going to add that final time, the total time elapsed, at a certain position on our plot.

538
00:53:36,046 --> 00:53:38,820
To start, we're going to define a message variable.

539
00:53:39,278 --> 00:53:42,404
This message variable gets used by the text function.

540
00:53:42,830 --> 00:53:49,188
The text function takes a X position, a Y position, and a message and puts it on top of our figure.

541
00:53:50,190 --> 00:53:53,636
The message should read total time 20 seconds.

542
00:53:56,494 --> 00:54:03,236
The string cat function takes a bunch of arguments that are strings and makes them into one single string.

543
00:54:04,366 --> 00:54:07,812
Total time is a normal string, and so is seconds.

544
00:54:08,430 --> 00:54:13,636
We want to include the last value in the time vector as a string as well.

545
00:54:14,062 --> 00:54:25,220
So we're calling timeend and then converting it from a double to a string. So it can be included with these other strings inside the string cat function.

546
00:54:26,318 --> 00:54:31,716
We pass the message to the text command and it will be placed at these coordinates on our plot.

547
00:54:32,622 --> 00:54:36,196
When we run this, we now see our completed professional plot.

548
00:54:36,750 --> 00:54:43,268
You can see the very first value is in green at the bottom, the path continues all the way until the red square.

549
00:54:44,398 --> 00:54:48,836
The total time is displayed at these coordinates, and we have our legend in the bottom left.

550
00:54:49,486 --> 00:54:51,524
Our axes are labeled with a title.

551
00:54:52,398 --> 00:54:56,932
To save this, you can go to File, Save As, and give it a file name.

552
00:54:57,582 --> 00:55:01,540
Once you click save, it should appear in your current folder if that's where you saved it.

